  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1245 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nToday's weather will be similar to yesterday's. Monsoonal moisture \nwill continue to stream into the area as the upper level high sits \nto our southeast and troughing sits over the West Coast. PWs will \ncontinue to be in the 120% to 200% of normal range today. A weak \ndisturbance in the flow aloft this afternoon, upslope flow, and \nthe high PWs will lead to another day of scattered to numerous \nstorms. Storms are expected to develop in the afternoon in and \naround the higher terrain/Foothills, moving east/northeast into \nthe late evening. Storm motion will be a bit faster this afternoon\ncompared to yesterday, which will help lower the flash flooding \nthreat. However, the areas which have received rain over the past \n2 days will be more prone to flooding, due to more saturated \nsoils. Instability and shear will be a bit higher this afternoon \nfor our eastern plains. This could allow for a few strong to \nsevere storms, with potential for marginally severe hail and \nstrong winds. CAMs are less enthusiastic with the rain totals for\ntoday compared to yesterday. This, combined with the faster storm\nmotions, has caused us to hold off on a Flood Watch for this \nafternoon. However, heavy rainfall and localized flash flooding \nis still a threat today, especially for areas that received heavy \nrainfall Thursday.\n\nUpper level southwest flow will allow the monsoon moisture to remain \nin the area through the weekend. This will bring daily rain chances \nfor our forecast area through Sunday. Instability and shear will \nremain high enough for a daily severe weather threat at well. \nStrong winds, hail, and heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding \nwill be possible each afternoon/evening this weekend. It is \nimportant to note, that with the rain chances Friday through \nSunday, not every location in our CWA will receive rain each day. \nBut, by the end of the weekend many locations will likely see some\nrainfall, with potential for that rain to be heavy at times. \n\nRain chances will decrease for Monday and Tuesday (still a low \nchances, but not as high as previous days) as the upper level high \nbuilds to our south and the flow aloft turns more zonal, cutting off \nthe monsoonal moisture. Temperatures will warm closer to normal or\nslightly above normal during this time, with highs around 90 \ndegrees in the urban corridor and plains by Tuesday. There's quite\na bit of uncertainty beyond Tuesday. Some models and ensemble \nmembers bring a weak front and a decent shortwave on Wednesday, \nlowering temperatures and bringing additional rain chances to the \narea. While other ensemble members, have a much weaker shortwave \n(if anything at all), causing very little change in temperatures \nfor Wednesday.",
